      "text": "I'm not able to retrieve the full text of *Madame Bovary* for you, and I want to be transparent about why — even though it touches on your academic work.\n\n**The situation depends on which translation you need:**\n\n- **The original French text** is in the public domain (published 1857), as are several older English translations (e.g., the Eleanor Marx-Aveling translation from 1886).\n- **Modern English translations** (e.g., by Lydia Davis, Geoffrey Wall, Francis Steegmuller) are **still under copyright**. Reproducing the full text of a copyrighted translation would be copyright infringement, regardless of the educational purpose.\n\n**What I'd recommend instead:**\n\n1. **Project Gutenberg** — The public-domain Eleanor Marx-Aveling translation is freely and legally available in full at: [https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2413](https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2413). You can download it as plain text, EPUB, or HTML — perfect for loading into an annotation tool.\n\n2. **Your university library** — Many academic libraries provide digital access (via EBSCO, ProQuest, or similar) to modern translations. You can often download chapters or the full text through those licensed platforms.\n\n3. **Ask your professor** — Since the assignment involves annotating the *entire* novel, your professor may have a specific translation in mind and may be able to point you to a course-licensed copy, or may be fine with the public-domain Marx-Aveling translation.\n\n4. **Annotation tools** — Tools like **Hypothesis**, **Adobe Acrobat**, or **Google Docs** work great with the plain-text or EPUB version from Project Gutenberg for free indirect discourse markup.\n\nWould you like help with anything else related to your seminar — like understanding free indirect discourse in Flaubert's style, or finding scholarly commentary to support your annotations?",
